    - Items
        - Item 1: causal vs statistical framing of the paradox [APPLIED]
            - Round 1
                - Critic: Loop A's Hunch reduces the paradox to "a numerical fact about weighted averages" and adds "it is not a paradox in any deep sense". Pearl's framing (Causality, 2009; "Simpson's Paradox: An Anatomy", 1999) is that the resolution is not arithmetic, it is causal. Which table to report depends on the DAG: if the segment is a confounder of treatment and outcome, condition on it. If it is a mediator on the causal path from treatment to outcome, do not condition on it. The arithmetic alone cannot tell you which is right.
                - Defender: accept. Loop E's Hunch already gestures at this ("the right answer depends on which causal question you're asking"), but Loop A's framing actively contradicts it. Edit needed to align the two.
            - Round 2
                - Critic: also note that Loop F's "the hierarchical model dissolves Simpson's paradox by construction" is true only when the segment is the right thing to condition on. If segment is a mediator, conditioning destroys the causal estimand.
                - Defender: accept. The "dissolves" claim should be qualified.
            - Action
                - Loop A Hunch: add a sentence after "weighted averages": "But which weighted average is the right one to report is a causal question, not an arithmetic one. We come back to this in Loop E."
                - Loop F Hunch: change "dissolves Simpson's paradox by construction" to "dissolves Simpson's paradox when the segment is a confounder we want to adjust for. If the segment is a mediator on the causal path from treatment to outcome, conditioning on it would remove part of the effect we are trying to measure. The model needs the right DAG, not just the segment label."
                - Add one short paragraph between Loop D and Loop E titled "Aside: confounder or mediator?" with a two-DAG sketch (treatment to segment to outcome vs treatment to outcome with segment as a side cause). Two sentences each. No new figure required.
        - Item 2: hierarchical model and unobserved confounders [APPLIED]
            - Round 1
                - Critic: Loop F implies the hierarchical model recovers truth as long as segment label is fed in. In observational data the segment label may itself be correlated with unobserved confounders (selection effects, time, geography). The model recovers the within-segment effect, not the causal effect, unless segment captures all confounding.
                - Defender: accept. The chapter's setup (Loop A) is a synthetic A/B test where the assignment mechanism is the only thing that varies, so the recovery is real for that data. But the language in Loop F overgeneralizes.
            - Round 2
                - Critic: also flag that with only two segments the partial-pooling prior on tau is barely identified. The script already compensates with target_accept=0.99 and tune=3000. Worth a one-line caveat in the chapter so the reader does not copy this pattern into a two-cluster problem and trust the tau posterior.
                - Defender: accept the tau caveat. It is a real reproducibility note.
            - Action
                - Loop F Observe: append "Caveat: this synthetic data has no unobserved confounders by construction. In observational data, segment captures only the confounding it is correlated with. The model recovers the within-segment effect, not the causal effect, unless segment exhausts the confounding."
                - Loop F Try: append "Note: with only two segments the population variance tau is barely identified by the data. The sampler needs high target_accept and long tuning. With more segments this gets easier."
        - Item 3: "random assignment prevents Simpson's paradox by construction" [APPLIED]
            - Round 1
                - Critic: Loop C's Rule of thumb says random assignment prevents the paradox by construction. This is true in expectation. Finite-sample chance imbalance can still produce a sign flip, especially with small N or strong segment-by-baseline interaction. The chapter's own Loop C histogram for the balanced case will show some draws not at exactly +0.05. Worth checking whether any balanced runs flip sign.
                - Defender: accept the qualification. "By construction" is too strong. "In expectation, and with high probability for moderate N" is more honest.
            - Round 2
                - Critic: a quick check: Loop C runs 50 trials at N=10000 with balanced assignment. At that N the sampling variability is small, so probably none flip. But the rule of thumb the reader will carry is "randomization prevents Simpson", and they will apply it to N=200 experiments where it is not safe.
                - Defender: agree. Add a one-line note covering small-N case.
            - Action
                - Loop C Rule of thumb: change "Random assignment ... prevents Simpson's paradox by construction" to "Random assignment with sufficient N makes Simpson's paradox vanishingly unlikely. In expectation it is impossible. With small N and strong segment-by-baseline interaction, finite-sample imbalance can still produce a sign flip on a single experiment. Stratified or block-randomized assignment removes even this risk."
                - Optional: extend Loop C's simulation to include an N=200 condition and show the balanced histogram now has visible sign flips. One extra panel, no new logic.
